{
  "title": "Diphthongs, breathings and accents",
  "note": "Two vowels can blend into one syllable, a diphthong; its breathing and accent sit on the second vowel: εὐ, αἱ. Initial ρ and υ always take a rough breathing. A small ι under a long vowel (ᾳ ῃ ῳ) is iota subscript, silent when read aloud. Words are cited alone with an acute; in running text a final acute becomes grave (´ → `). ; is the question mark.",
